WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:04.800
All right, friends, so now let's say that you have created your indexes in your database and your

00:04.800 --> 00:10.160
query is optimized and you have fast performance, but the job is not done yet.

00:10.480 --> 00:11.960
No God.

00:13.400 --> 00:14.000
No.

00:14.000 --> 00:14.920
God, please.

00:14.920 --> 00:17.240
No, no, no.

00:17.800 --> 00:25.440
Because over the time the indexes get fragmented, outdated, unused and this can lead to a poor performance

00:25.440 --> 00:29.200
in your queries and as well can increase the storage costs.

00:29.240 --> 00:33.080
And the overall performance of your database cannot drop down.

00:33.080 --> 00:36.400
So indexes like having a car, it needs maintenance.

00:36.400 --> 00:39.480
So you need to change the oil and the tire of the car.

00:39.760 --> 00:41.800
And the same thing goes for the indexes.

00:42.040 --> 00:43.120
You have to maintain them.

00:43.120 --> 00:46.920
They need attention to keep everything running smoothly.

00:46.920 --> 00:52.520
So now I'm going to show you how I manage, maintain and monitor the indexes of my SQL projects.

00:52.560 --> 00:53.320
So let's go.

00:57.520 --> 01:02.090
The first and the most important task is to monitor the usage of your indexes.

01:02.290 --> 01:07.570
So of course, the first question we have to ask ourselves over the time are we using really the indexes

01:07.570 --> 01:09.090
that you have created?

01:09.090 --> 01:14.690
Are they really helping improving the speed of my queries, or was it just a good idea at the start

01:14.690 --> 01:18.290
of the project and later no one used those indexes?

01:18.290 --> 01:24.330
This is very crucial because if you are having an unused index, you are consuming unnecessary storage

01:24.330 --> 01:30.890
space and as well, the right performance in the tables can be slow, which is completely unnecessary

01:30.890 --> 01:32.450
if you are not using the index.

01:32.450 --> 01:37.890
So now our task is to find out the usage of each index that we have in the projects.

01:37.890 --> 01:39.290
So let's see how we can do that.

01:39.290 --> 01:44.090
So now we have created like multiple indexes on that table DB customers.

01:44.090 --> 01:48.450
So if you go to the DB customers and to the indexes you can see that we have four indexes.

01:48.490 --> 01:54.410
Now you can go and show those informations by using a special stored procedures from the SQL server

01:54.410 --> 01:56.090
called SP Help Index.

01:56.090 --> 01:56.970
Let's go and do that.

01:56.970 --> 02:00.420
So ESP help index.

02:00.620 --> 02:04.220
So it is a system stored procedure that comes with the database.

02:04.340 --> 02:08.580
So this stored procedure needs only one value and that is the table name.

02:08.580 --> 02:11.980
So we have it over here sales db customers.

02:12.020 --> 02:13.660
Let's go and query it.

02:13.660 --> 02:15.060
So we have four indexes.

02:15.060 --> 02:17.340
Then we have a nice description of the index.

02:17.340 --> 02:19.540
So it says it is non-clustered index.

02:19.540 --> 02:22.980
And whether it is columnstore and let's say where it is located.

02:22.980 --> 02:25.580
So it says it's located on primary.

02:25.620 --> 02:29.020
Primary is the name of the file group where the data is stored.

02:29.020 --> 02:31.420
And as a default can be stored as primary.

02:31.420 --> 02:33.980
And now the next information we have the index keys.

02:34.020 --> 02:39.660
It is nice information to understand which keys are used or which columns are used for the index.

02:39.660 --> 02:41.700
So the first one you can see we have two columns.

02:41.700 --> 02:43.900
That means it is a composite index.

02:43.900 --> 02:46.700
And of course for the column store we don't have any columns.

02:46.700 --> 02:48.460
And then we have the first name last name.

02:48.460 --> 02:53.260
So this is a really nice quick stored procedure in order to see information about our index okay.

02:53.260 --> 02:57.510
So now let's focus on our task on how to monitor the usage of the indexes.

02:57.550 --> 03:04.910
Now, in databases we have a lot of schemas and tables that protocol the metadata of our database.

03:04.910 --> 03:10.550
And in SQL server we have a special schema called SSIS where you can find a lot of metadata information

03:10.550 --> 03:16.870
about the SQL server metadata like the description of the tables, views, columns, and as well the

03:16.870 --> 03:17.550
indexes.

03:17.550 --> 03:21.550
So now let's check what we can find inside the table indexes.

03:21.550 --> 03:22.310
So let's go and do it.

03:22.350 --> 03:24.310
Select star from SSIS.

03:24.350 --> 03:25.710
This is the schema name.

03:26.230 --> 03:29.150
And then as you can see we have a list of many informations.

03:29.150 --> 03:31.470
But we want to focus on the indexes.

03:31.510 --> 03:33.390
Now let's go and execute it.

03:33.390 --> 03:40.590
Now we get a huge list of all indexes that we have and a lot of informations for each index.

03:40.590 --> 03:45.110
We don't have to go and understand now each column, but I'm going to go and select the main important

03:45.110 --> 03:47.110
informations from this table.

03:47.110 --> 03:47.830
So what do we need.

03:47.830 --> 03:48.710
The object ID.

03:48.750 --> 03:54.230
This is the table id so the object id and we have the name.

03:54.430 --> 03:56.280
It is the index name.

03:57.720 --> 04:02.400
And then here we have a nice information whether it is clustered or non-clustered.

04:02.400 --> 04:03.680
So let's go and select it.

04:03.720 --> 04:05.680
Type disk as.

04:05.800 --> 04:07.600
So let's call it index type.

04:07.640 --> 04:10.480
And we can go and check whether it is primary key or not.

04:10.480 --> 04:15.560
So let's get this information as well is primary key I will go and just rename.

04:15.560 --> 04:18.040
It is primary key.

04:18.200 --> 04:20.320
And what else do we need whether it is unique.

04:20.320 --> 04:22.560
So it is as well nice information to have.

04:22.800 --> 04:24.000
So is unique.

04:28.040 --> 04:30.320
So of course you can go and grab a lot of stuff.

04:30.320 --> 04:32.560
It depends really on what you are monitoring.

04:32.560 --> 04:35.480
So for example I'm going to go and check whether it is disabled or not.

04:35.480 --> 04:39.920
So is disabled and I'll just rename it.

04:42.480 --> 04:44.520
So with that I have like focus monitoring.

04:44.520 --> 04:46.200
I don't have to have all those informations.

04:46.200 --> 04:47.400
So let's go and execute.

04:47.400 --> 04:49.320
But now I would like to go and change a few stuff.

04:49.320 --> 04:54.210
Like for example I don't want the object ID, I would like to have the full name of the table.

04:54.210 --> 04:59.130
And as well there is a lot of indexes that is relevant for my database.

04:59.130 --> 05:03.610
So now in order to do that, we have to go and get the informations from another metadata table.

05:03.610 --> 05:08.890
So let's go and call this index and let's go and join it with another metadata table.

05:09.810 --> 05:11.170
It's called tables.

05:11.330 --> 05:15.130
So TBL and we're going to go and join it using the.

05:15.170 --> 05:21.570
So the index object ID equal to the table object ID.

05:22.610 --> 05:27.370
And now if you'd like to see the content of this table we can go and create separately.

05:27.370 --> 05:30.810
So select star from our new table.

05:31.290 --> 05:33.090
So let's see the content of this table.

05:33.090 --> 05:35.610
So you can see we have the name which is the table name.

05:35.610 --> 05:37.010
And I think that's all what we need.

05:37.010 --> 05:39.490
We have a lot of other informations about the table.

05:39.650 --> 05:41.370
Well I just need the table name.

05:41.370 --> 05:46.650
So let's go and do it at the start table name as table name.

05:48.010 --> 05:50.330
And I don't need any more the object ID.

05:50.370 --> 05:54.980
But of course we have to go and use the alias for each of those informations in order to understand

05:54.980 --> 05:57.180
those informations comes from the index.

05:57.180 --> 05:59.100
So let's go and do that.

05:59.140 --> 05:59.620
All right.

05:59.620 --> 06:00.980
So my query is ready.

06:00.980 --> 06:02.500
Let's go and execute it again.

06:02.540 --> 06:04.660
So now as you can see we are getting the table name.

06:04.660 --> 06:10.020
And the list is very short because it is only focusing on the tables that you have in the database.

06:10.020 --> 06:12.420
And this filter happens because of the inner join.

06:12.420 --> 06:14.780
But one more thing I would like to go and sort the data.

06:14.780 --> 06:15.980
So I'm going to say order by.

06:15.980 --> 06:21.460
I would like to sort it by the table name and then the index name.

06:21.460 --> 06:22.060
All right.

06:22.100 --> 06:24.820
So now let's go and check for example the table customers.

06:24.820 --> 06:27.380
You can see that we have two non-clustered index.

06:27.380 --> 06:29.500
And one of them is Columnstore index.

06:29.500 --> 06:31.820
Those two we have created from the previous tutorial.

06:31.820 --> 06:34.060
And we have an index on the primary key.

06:34.060 --> 06:36.380
As you can see here is primary key equal to one.

06:36.380 --> 06:38.260
And this is as well unique.

06:38.260 --> 06:43.260
So with that we have a really nice list of all indexes that we have in our database.

06:43.260 --> 06:49.020
But we are not there yet because our task is how to monitor the usage of the index.

06:49.020 --> 06:55.870
Now in order to get the usage For each of those indexes, we have to go to a special view called dynamic

06:55.870 --> 06:56.750
Management view.

06:56.790 --> 07:01.150
And there the SQL server can provide a lot of statistics about the usage for that index.

07:01.150 --> 07:03.110
And we can find it as well in the same schema.

07:03.390 --> 07:05.430
So let's go and query this table.

07:05.430 --> 07:07.710
So it's going to be select star from.

07:07.870 --> 07:15.670
So the same schema is.dm underscore db underscore index usage stats.

07:15.870 --> 07:19.110
So let's go and explore this table and execute it.

07:19.150 --> 07:24.950
Now in those statistics we can find the usage of two indexes the index number three and one.

07:24.950 --> 07:28.910
And we can see there are like three usage informations of that index number one.

07:28.910 --> 07:33.070
And next we have like user six user scans and user lookups.

07:33.150 --> 07:37.750
So this is how many times the index is used as sex or scans or lookups.

07:37.990 --> 07:41.670
We will understand those informations as we learn about the execution plan.

07:41.670 --> 07:46.630
And here we have a very nice information about how many times our index got updated.

07:46.630 --> 07:51.920
So as you can see here is zero because I didn't add any new data after creating the index.

07:51.920 --> 07:56.120
But of course, all those numbers might be different at your side because it depends whether you are

07:56.120 --> 07:58.000
doing more queries and practicing.

07:58.000 --> 08:04.960
And you can find here more informations about when was exactly the last usage of those indexes and many,

08:04.960 --> 08:06.560
many nice informations.

08:06.560 --> 08:10.160
So now let's go and integrate this view with our query.

08:10.160 --> 08:12.640
So now what I'm going to do I'm going to do a left join.

08:12.640 --> 08:17.760
Because if I do an inner join I will only find the used indexes.

08:17.760 --> 08:22.360
But I don't want that because I want to see a full build of all my indexes in the database.

08:22.360 --> 08:28.120
So left join and we're going to go and get our view and call it's.

08:28.120 --> 08:30.360
And then we have to join it on the keys.

08:30.360 --> 08:31.440
So s on.

08:31.440 --> 08:36.440
So let's say let's go and grab the object ID equal to the index object ID.

08:37.400 --> 08:40.680
And of course we have to join on the index ID.

08:40.680 --> 08:46.480
So it's going to be the index id equal to the index id like this.

08:46.480 --> 08:49.730
Now we have to go and select few informations from this view.

08:49.730 --> 08:52.530
So I'm going to go and select like all those.

08:52.570 --> 08:53.610
Number of usage.

08:53.610 --> 09:02.770
So s let's get the user six s the user scans and the lookups.

09:04.010 --> 09:06.930
And maybe as well the user updates.

09:07.210 --> 09:11.090
And it is really nice information to understand when it was the last time used.

09:11.090 --> 09:17.370
So last user seek and the last user scan.

09:17.650 --> 09:19.210
Let me just correct it over here.

09:19.210 --> 09:22.530
And actually I can go and put those two dates in one date.

09:22.530 --> 09:27.650
Because if it's like the last week it's going to be null over here or the opposite.

09:27.690 --> 09:31.610
And now what we can do, we can go and put those two together actually in one column.

09:31.610 --> 09:35.050
Because when we have a value over here, it's going to be null and vice versa.

09:35.050 --> 09:39.450
So we can do that using the null function coalesce like this.

09:39.450 --> 09:44.050
And we can get this over here and we can call the whole thing last update.

09:45.810 --> 09:46.900
So like this.

09:46.940 --> 09:49.260
And maybe I'm going to go and rename all those stuff.

09:56.620 --> 09:57.020
All right.

09:57.020 --> 09:58.140
So now we are done.

09:58.140 --> 09:59.660
Let's go and execute it.

09:59.700 --> 10:00.060
Okay.

10:00.100 --> 10:02.660
So let's go and check our new reports over here.

10:02.660 --> 10:03.980
So this is our query.

10:03.980 --> 10:08.020
And let's start with the first table for example the customers and go to the right side.

10:08.060 --> 10:10.460
And now we can see that we have three indexes.

10:10.660 --> 10:15.700
And from these two indexes we have only one index that is not used at all.

10:15.700 --> 10:21.460
So we can see over here that the non-clustered index on the country is not being used.

10:21.460 --> 10:26.300
And that's because we have another index above the country that comes from the column store.

10:26.300 --> 10:30.500
So it could be like this that you are querying the table using the country.

10:30.500 --> 10:35.860
But the SQL saying, I would like to go and use this index instead of the first one, so we can say,

10:35.900 --> 10:37.980
okay, this one is not really useful.

10:38.020 --> 10:39.820
Maybe we can go and drop it, right?

10:39.820 --> 10:45.230
And for the rest you can see, okay, this Columnstore index is used twice and the next one is Once

10:45.270 --> 10:50.030
again, the numbers at your side might be different, and if you have a look to all other tables, we

10:50.030 --> 10:51.310
have a lot of nulls.

10:51.310 --> 10:55.710
So that means all those indexes that we have created on the DB customers.

10:55.710 --> 10:56.550
Let me check.

10:56.590 --> 10:57.630
Only one is used.

10:57.630 --> 10:59.830
But now you might say you know what, I've used the index.

10:59.830 --> 11:00.190
But why.

11:00.190 --> 11:02.070
I'm not seeing here any numbers about it.

11:02.110 --> 11:05.630
Well that's because those numbers will not live forever.

11:05.870 --> 11:09.590
And we are using now the Express Edition locally at our PC.

11:09.590 --> 11:14.150
So each time you shut down your PC and you close the client, that database is going to shut down as

11:14.150 --> 11:14.470
well.

11:14.470 --> 11:17.750
And those statistics are going to be lost because they are in the memory.

11:17.750 --> 11:21.310
But in real projects, the number is going to be totally different than here.

11:21.310 --> 11:23.470
And of course you're going to get realistic numbers.

11:23.510 --> 11:27.230
Now let's try to target one of those not used indexes.

11:27.230 --> 11:29.150
So for example let's go with this index.

11:29.150 --> 11:31.670
It is not clustered index on the products.

11:31.670 --> 11:32.830
So let's go and query that.

11:32.830 --> 11:34.830
Currently it is completely not used.

11:34.830 --> 11:46.400
So if I go and select it so select star from sales products where products equal to cups.

11:46.600 --> 11:49.360
So with that we have used the index I think.

11:49.400 --> 11:51.800
Let's go back and query again.

11:51.840 --> 11:55.360
And let's go to our index and check whether it is used.

11:55.360 --> 11:56.360
Well it is correct.

11:56.360 --> 11:58.400
So our query did use this index.

11:58.400 --> 12:00.680
And we can see here it is used once.

12:00.720 --> 12:05.200
And now you can go and analyze in your project all the indexes that you have on your tables.

12:05.480 --> 12:09.560
And you can see whether you are really using it with your queries or not.

12:09.560 --> 12:13.400
And if you are not using the query, of course you have to make a decision about it.

12:13.440 --> 12:17.400
Maybe if you are working in a team to ask about it, who did create it and why.

12:17.520 --> 12:22.120
Maybe there is like one task in the database that is not frequently used.

12:22.160 --> 12:25.600
Maybe it's something that is run like once a month or something like that.

12:25.600 --> 12:28.120
So the index is needed, but not that frequently.

12:28.120 --> 12:33.480
But still now we have like insights about what is going on with those indexes and whether we need them

12:33.480 --> 12:34.120
or not.

12:34.160 --> 12:36.800
And if you don't need them, go and drop them.

12:36.840 --> 12:37.680
All right, my friends.

12:37.680 --> 12:42.650
So here is the secret that 90% of SQL developers don't do that.

12:42.650 --> 12:45.810
It's gonna make you in one minute the hero of the project.

12:45.850 --> 12:51.090
So once I joined a project and after saying hello to everyone, I opened the database of the project

12:51.090 --> 12:52.770
and do one query.

12:52.810 --> 12:58.410
I checked the usage of the indexes of the projects, and I can tell you, after working 15 years with

12:58.450 --> 13:05.290
SQL, that 90% of indexes created in projects are totally untouched and unused.

13:05.370 --> 13:09.050
So I collect all unused indexes and discuss it with the team.

13:09.050 --> 13:13.930
And if we don't find a real usage for those indexes, we go and drop them.

13:14.010 --> 13:19.490
So after dropping all those unused indexes, you have done two great things for the projects.

13:19.610 --> 13:22.890
First, you have saved a lot of storage in the database.

13:22.890 --> 13:28.970
And second, which is way more important, you have improved and optimized the right performance on

13:28.970 --> 13:29.890
the database.

13:30.010 --> 13:35.370
So in your first day with one query, you have optimized the performance of the database, you have

13:35.370 --> 13:39.130
saved storage and you can shine like an expert in your projects.

13:39.170 --> 13:41.290
So if you haven't done that do that now.
